_model: proposition
---
title: Proposition 54
---
order: 54
---
short_label: X.54
---
statement: <b><a href="propX54.html#lemma">Lemma.</a></b>
---
proof:

<h3 id="lemma">Lemma</h3>

Let there be two squares *AB* and *BC*, and let them be placed so that *DB* is in a straight line with *BE*.  Then *FB* is also in a straight line with *BG*.  Complete the parallelogram *AC*.

I say that *AC* is a square, that *DG* is a mean proportional between *AB* and *BC*, and further that *DC* is a mean proportional between *AC* and *CB*.

Since *DB* equals *BF*, and *BE* is to *BG*, therefore the whole *DE* equals the whole *FG*.

<figure class="diagram">
<canvas id="canvas_0" width="260" height="260" tabindex="0"></canvas>
<noscript><img alt="X.54 Lemma diagram" src="propX54l.gif"/></noscript>
<script type="text/javascript">
    geomlib.init({
        canvasid: "canvas_0",
        background: "35,19,100",
        title: "Lemma",
        elements: [
            "X;point;free;1000,0;0,0",
            "Y;point;free;0,-1000;0,0",
            "O;point;free;0,0;0;0",
            "Z-;point;free;-100,360;0;0",
            "Z+;point;free;360,-100;0;0",
            "A;point;lineSlider;Z-,Z+,40,210",
            "B;point;lineSlider;Z-,Z+,160,80",
            "C;point;lineSlider;Z-,Z+,210,30",
            "KA;line;parallel;A,O,Y;0;0;0",
            "AH;line;parallel;A,O,X;0;0;0",
            "GF;line;parallel;B,O,Y;0;0;0",
            "DE;line;parallel;B,O,X;0;0;0",
            "CH;line;parallel;C,O,Y;0;0;0",
            "KC;line;parallel;C,O,X;0;0;0",
            "D;point;intersection;KA,DE",
            "E;point;intersection;CH,DE",
            "F;point;intersection;AH,GF",
            "G;point;intersection;KC,GF",
            "H;point;intersection;AH,CH",
            "K;point;intersection;KC,KA",
            "AKCH;polygon;quadrilateral;A,K,C,H;0;0;black;random",
            "ADBF;polygon;quadrilateral;A,D,B,F;0;0;black;random",
            "BECG;polygon;quadrilateral;B,E,C,G;0;0;black;random"
        ],
    });
</script>
</figure>

But *DE* equals each of the straight lines *AH* and *KC*, and *FG* equals each of the straight lines *AK* and *HC*, therefore each of the straight lines *AH* and *KC* also equals each of the straight lines *AK* and *HC*. [!just I.34]

Therefore the parallelogram *AC* is equilateral. And it is also rectangular, therefore *AC* is a square.

Since *FB* is to *BG* as *DB* is to *BE*, while *FB* is to *BG* as *AB* is to *DG*, and *DB* is to *BE* as *DG* is to *BC*, therefore *AB* is to *DG* as *DG* is to *BC*. [!just VI.1; VI.11]

Therefore *DG* is a mean proportional between *AB* and *BC*.

I say next that *DC* is also a mean proportional between *AC* and *CB*.

Since *AD* is to *DK* as *KG* is to *GC*, for they are equal respectively, and, taken jointly, *AK* is to *KD* as *KC* is to *CG*, while *AK* is to *KD* as *AC* is to *CD*, and *AC* is to *CG* as *DC* is to *CB*, therefore *AC* is to *DC* as *DC* is to *BC*. [!just V.18; VI.1; VI.11]

Therefore *DC* is a mean proportional between *AC* and *CB*.

<div class="qed">Q.E.D.</div>

### Proposition 54

If an area is contained by a rational straight line and the first binomial, then the side of the area is the irrational straight line which is called binomial.

Let the area *AC* be contained by the rational straight line *AB* and the first binomial *AD*.

I say that the side of the area *AC* is the irrational straight line which is called binomial.

Since *AD* is a first binomial straight line, divide it into its terms at *E*, and let *AE* be the greater term.

<figure class="diagram">
<canvas id="canvas_1" width="400" height="200" tabindex="0"></canvas>
<noscript><img alt="X.54 diagram" src="propX54.gif"/></noscript>
<script type="text/javascript">
    geomlib.init({
        canvasid: "canvas_1",
        background: "35,19,100",
        title: "X.54",
        elements: [
            "A0;point;free;-1000,60;0;0",
            "A3;point;free;1000,60;0;0",
            "A;point;lineSlider;A0,A3,30,0",
            "D;point;lineSlider;A0,A3,200,0",
            "B';point;perpendicular;A,D;0;0",
            "B;point;lineSlider;A,B',0,150",
            "C;point;parallelogram;B,A,D",
            "ABCD;polygon;quadrilateral;A,B,C,D;0;0;black;random",
            "E;point;lineSlider;A,D,120,0",
            "F;point;midpoint;E,D",
            "AEmid;point;midpoint;A,E;0;0",
            "AEquar;point;midpoint;E,AEmid;0;0",
            "EFcirc;circle;radius;E,F;0;0;0;0",
            "EAcirc;circle;radius;AEquar,E;0;0;0;0",
            "bich;line;bichord;EFcirc,EAcirc;0;0;0",
            "G';point;first;bich;0;0",
            "G;point;cutoff;AEmid,D,AEmid,G'",
            "H;point;parallelogram;B,A,G",
            "K;point;parallelogram;B,A,E",
            "L;point;parallelogram;B,A,F",
            "HGEK;polygon;quadrilateral;H,G,E,K;0;0;black;90,80,100",
            "FL;line;connect;F,L",
            "AH;polygon;quadrilateral;A,B,H,G;0;0;0;30,80,100",
            "S0;point;free;-1000,175;0;0",
            "S3;point;free;1000,175;0;0",
            "S;point;lineSlider;S0,S3,240,0",
            "P;point;meanProportional;A,G,G,H,S,S3",
            "SPNM;polygon;square;S,P;0;0;black;30,80,100",
            "M;point;vertex;SPNM,4",
            "N;point;vertex;SPNM,3",
            "T;point;meanProportional;G,E,E,K,P,S3;0",
            "O;point;parallelogram;M,S,T",
            "NOQR;polygon;square;N,O;0;0;black;90,80,100",
            "Q;point;vertex;NOQR,3",
            "R;point;vertex;NOQR,4",
            "SQ;polygon;parallelogram;S,T,Q;0;0;black;0"
        ],
    });
</script>
</figure>

It is then manifest that *AE* and *ED* are rational straight lines commensurable in square only, the square on *AE* is greater than the square on *ED* by the square on a straight line commensurable with *AE*, and *AE* is commensurable in length with the rational straight line *AB* set out. [!just X.Def.II.1]

Bisect *ED* at the point *F*.

Then, since the square on *AE* is greater than the square on *ED* by the square on a straight line commensurable with *AE*, therefore, if there is applied to the greater *AE* a parallelogram equal to the fourth part of the square on the less, that is, to the square on *EF*, and deficient by a square figure, then it divides it into commensurable parts. [!just X.17]

Apply to *AE* the rectangle *AG* by *GE* equal to the square on *EF*.  Then *AG* is commensurable in length with *EG*.

<div class="just"><a href="/elements/books/bookII/propositions/propII4/">II.4</a><br><a href="#lemma">Lemma</a></div>
Draw *GH*, *EK*, and *FL* from *G*, *E*, and *F* parallel to either of the straight lines *AB* and *CD*.  Construct the square *SN* equal to the parallelogram *AH*, and the square *NQ* equal to *GK*, and place them so that *MN* is in a straight line with *NO*.  Then *RN* is also in a straight line with *NP*.  Complete the parallelogram *SQ*. Then *SQ* is a square.

Now, since the rectangle *AG* by *GE* equals the square on *EF*, therefore *AG* is to *EF* as *FE* is to *EG*. [!just VI.17]

Therefore *AH* is to *EL* as *EL* is to *KG*. Therefore *EL* is a mean proportional between *AH* and *GK*. [!just VI.1]

<div class="just"><a href="#lemma">Lemma</a></div>
But *AH* equals *SN*, and *GK* equals *NQ*, therefore *EL* is a mean proportional between *SN* and *NQ*. But *MR* is also a mean proportional between the same *SN* and *NQ*, therefore *EL* equals *MR*, so that it also equals *PO*.

But *AH* and *GK* also equal *SN* and *NQ*, therefore the whole *AC* equals the whole *SQ*, that is, it equals the square on *MO*, Therefore *MO* is the side of *AC*.

I say next that *MO* is binomial.

Since *AG* is commensurable with *GE*, therefore *AE* is also commensurable with each of the straight lines *AG* and *GE*. [!just X.15]

But *AE* is also, by hypothesis, commensurable with *AB*, therefore *AG* and *GE* are also commensurable with *AB*. [!just X.12]

And *AB* is rational, therefore each of the straight lines *AG* and *GE* is also rational. Therefore each of the rectangles *AH* and *GK* is rational, and *AH* is commensurable with *GK*. [!just X.19]

But *AH* equals *SN*, and *GK* equals *NQ*, therefore the sum of *SN* and *NQ*, that is the squares on *MN* and *NO*, are rational and commensurable.

Since *AE* is incommensurable in length with *ED*, while *AE* is commensurable with *AG*, and *DE* is commensurable with *EF*, therefore *AG* is also incommensurable with *EF*, so that *AH* is also incommensurable with *EL*. [!just X.13; VI.1, X.11]

But *AH* equals *SN*, and *EL* equals *MR*, therefore *SN* is also incommensurable with *MR*. But *SN* is to *MR* as *PN* is to *NR*, therefore *PN* is incommensurable with *NR*. [!just VI.1; X.11]

But *PN* equals *MN*, and *NR* equals *NO*, therefore *MN* is incommensurable with *NO*. And the square on *MN* is commensurable with the square on *NO*, and each is rational, therefore *MN* and *NO* are rational straight lines commensurable in square only.

Therefore *MO* is binomial and the side of *AC*. [!just X.36]

Therefore, *if an area is contained by a rational straight line and the first binomial, then the side of the area is the irrational straight line which is called binomial.*

<div class="qed">Q.E.D.</div>
---
body:

The lemma before the proposition is used in this proposition, @X.60, and @X.91. The proposition itself is used in @X.71.
